f=bnL,kFa=d$U,kFb=bnL,kE$=d$U,kFc=bnL,kE_=dE0,kFd=d_3,kE9=dE0,kFe=d_3,kFg=bnL,kE7="Current node is not a let or type alias expression, so no pattern to update",kE8=dAs,kE6="node-map",kGy=[0,1,0,0,1,0,S5,0,0,1,1,1,1,0],kGz=[0,0],kGw=m,kG6=[0,0,0],kG2=[0,0],kG1=[0,0,0],kGZ=[0,m],kG0=[0,m],kG3="Select not implemented for goals",kG4=[1,3],kG5=[1,3],kG7=[1,4],kG8=[0,1],kG9=[10,0],kG_=[10,1],kG$=[10,2],kHa=[6,[0,0,0]],kGH=b$,kGI=[0,[4,0,0,0,0],a9],kGJ=a4,kGK=bm,kGA=[0,[18,[1,[0,[11,b,0],b]],[11,aY,0]],aZ],kGB=ub,kGC=[0,[18,[1,[0,0,m]],[2,0,[11,v,[17,[0,a,1,0],0]]]],w],kGD=[0,[17,0,0],u],kGE=[0,[12,59,[17,[0,a,1,0],0]],A],kGF=aj7,kGG=[0,[18,[1,[0,0,m]],[2,0,[11,v,[17,[0,a,1,0],0]]]],w],kGL=[0,[17,0,0],u],kGM=[0,[17,[0,a,1,0],[12,az,[17,0,0]]],a0],kGV=aj7,kGW=ub,kGT=aj7,kGU=ub,kGR=[0,aj7],kGS=[0,ub],kGP=aj7,kGQ=ub,kGN=d5i,kHk=[1,"the library did not parse as module items"],kHi=[1,"the library must bind at least two names"],kHe=[0,[4,0,0,0,[11," static errors in the library",0]],"%d static errors in the library"],kHf=[1,"the library's context does not extend the builtins'"],kHg=[1,"the library did not elaborate"],kHh=[1,"no statics for the library's tail"],kHj=[1,"the library's top level may only hold definitions"],kHd=[1,"a top-level definition must bind exactly one name"],kHc=[1,"the library elaborated to an unexpected shape"],kHq=[0,b0_],kHr=[0,b07],kHn=b0_,kHo=b07,kHl="src/haz3lcore/zipper/ItemPersist.re.roster_entry",kHs='\nThe following demonstrates the ideal workflow pattern for implementing a user request.\nStudy this example carefully \xe2\x80\x94 it shows HOW to think, plan, and act.\n\n---\n\n### Example: User asks "Implement a fibonacci function and test it"\n\n**Starting program state:**\n```\n?\n```\n\n**Step 1 \xe2\x80\x94 Plan.** (Illustrates the workbench on a **multi-step feature**; skip this entirely for small/trivial edits.) Create a task with meaningful milestones (not micro-steps).\n\nTool call: `create_new_task`\n```json\n{\n  "task": {\n    "title": "Implement fibonacci with tests",\n    "description": "Implement a recursive fibonacci function and add test cases to verify correctness.",\n    "subtasks": [\n      {\n        "title": "Implement fibonacci function",\n        "description": "Define a recursive fib function: fib(0)=0, fib(1)=1, fib(n)=fib(n-1)+fib(n-2)."\n      },\n      {\n        "title": "Add test cases",\n        "description": "Add tests for base cases and a few recursive cases."\n      }\n    ]\n  }\n}\n```\n\n**Step 2 \xe2\x80\x94 Implement.** The program is empty (`?`), so use `insert_before` (or `insert_after`) with **no** `path` to seed the first binding. In real tasks, prefer a **minimal** first insert and then `insert_*` / `update_*` for the rest; this walkthrough uses one larger insert only to keep the transcript short.\n\nTool call: `insert_before`\n```json\n{\n  "code": "let fib(n: Int): Int =\\n  if n <= 0\\n    then 0\\n    else if n == 1\\n      then 1\\n      else fib(n - 1) + fib(n - 2)\\nin"\n}\n```\n\nAgent sees the updated program (via context):\n```\nlet fib(n: Int): Int =\n  if n <= 0\n    then 0\n    else if n == 1\n      then 1\n      else fib(n - 1) + fib(n - 2)\nin\n?\n```\nNo static errors. Mark first subtask complete:\nTool call: `mark_active_subtask_complete`\n```json\n{ "summary": "Implemented recursive fibonacci: fib(0)=0, fib(1)=1, fib(n)=fib(n-1)+fib(n-2)." }\n```\n\n**Step 3 \xe2\x80\x94 Add tests.** Update the body of `fib` (the `?` after `in`) with test expressions.\n\nTool call: `update_body`\n```json\n{\n  "path": "fib",\n  "code": "test fib(0) == 0 end;\\ntest fib(1) == 1 end;\\ntest fib(5) == 5 end;\\ntest fib(10) == 55 end;\\nfib(10)"\n}\n```\n\nAgent sees:\n```\nlet fib(n: Int): Int =\n  if n <= 0\n    then 0\n    else if n == 1\n      then 1\n      else fib(n - 1) + fib(n - 2)\nin\ntest fib(0) == 0 end;\ntest fib(1) == 1 end;\ntest fib(5) == 5 end;\ntest fib(10) == 55 end;\nfib(10)\n```\nNo static errors. All tests pass. Mark subtask and task complete:\nTool call: `mark_active_subtask_complete`\n```json\n{ "summary": "Added 4 test cases covering base cases (0, 1) and recursive cases (5, 10)." }\n```\nTool call: `mark_active_task_complete`\n```json\n{ "summary": "Implemented recursive fibonacci function with type annotation and 4 passing test cases." }\n```\n\n**Final message to user:** "The `fib` function is implemented and all 4 test cases pass. It handles base cases (0 and 1) and recursively computes fib(n) = fib(n-1) + fib(n-2) for larger values."\n\n---\n\n### Example: User asks "Add a helper function that computes fib for a list of numbers"\n\n**Starting program state:**\n```\nlet fib(n: Int): Int =\n  \xe2\x8b\xb1\nin\ntest fib(0) == 0 end;\ntest fib(1) == 1 end;\ntest fib(5) == 5 end;\ntest fib(10) == 55 end;\nfib(10)\n```\n\nNote: `fib` is collapsed (\xe2\x8b\xb1). The agent needs to understand what `fib` does but doesn\'t need to see its implementation \xe2\x80\x94 the tests provide enough context.\n\n**Step 1 \xe2\x80\x94 Plan.**\n\nTool call: `create_new_task`\n```json\n{\n  "task": {\n    "title": "Add map_fib helper",\n    "description": "Add a function that maps fib over a list of integers, plus test cases.",\n    "subtasks": [\n      {\n        "title": "Implement map_fib",\n        "description": "Insert a map_fib function after fib that applies fib to each element of an Int list."\n      },\n      {\n        "title": "Update program output with tests",\n        "description": "Replace the current program body with tests for map_fib and a demo call."\n      }\n    ]\n  }\n}\n```\n\n**Step 2 \xe2\x80\x94 Insert the new function.** Use `insert_after` to add a binding after `fib`.\n\nTool call: `insert_after`\n```json\n{\n  "path": "fib",\n  "code": "let map_fib(ns: [Int]): [Int] =\\n  case ns\\n  | [] => []\\n  | hd :: tl => fib(hd) :: map_fib(tl)\\n  end\\nin"\n}\n```\n\n**Step 3 \xe2\x80\x94 Update the body** to include tests for the new function.\n\nTool call: `update_body`\n```json\n{\n  "path": "map_fib",\n  "code": "test map_fib([]) == [] end;\\ntest map_fib([0, 1, 5]) == [0, 1, 5] end;\\nmap_fib([1, 2, 3, 4, 5])"\n}\n```\n\nNo static errors.\n\n**Step 4 \xe2\x80\x94 Verify with probes.** Use probes to confirm the output is correct.\n\nTool call: `place_probe`\n```json\n{ "paths": ["map_fib"] }\n```\n\nAgent sees (with probe results):\n```\nlet fib(n: Int): Int =\n  \xe2\x8b\xb1\nin\nlet map_fib(ns: [Int]): [Int] =\n  case ns\n  | [] => []                                   \xe2\x89\xa1 []\n  | hd :: tl => fib(hd) :: map_fib(tl)        \xe2\x89\xa1 [1, 1, 2, 3, 5]\n  end\nin\ntest map_fib([]) == [] end;\ntest map_fib([0, 1, 5]) == [0, 1, 5] end;\nmap_fib([1, 2, 3, 4, 5])                      \xe2\x89\xa1 [1, 1, 2, 3, 5]\n```\n\nThe `\xe2\x89\xa1` values confirm `map_fib` works correctly. Clean up the probe and mark complete:\nTool call: `remove_probe` \xe2\x86\x92 `mark_active_subtask_complete` \xe2\x86\x92 `mark_active_task_complete`\n\n**Final message to user:** "Added `map_fib` which maps the fibonacci function over a list. Verified with probes and 2 test cases."\n\n---\n\n### Key Patterns to Notice\n\n1. **Plan when it helps**: For big, multi-turn work, use coarse workbench milestones; for quick edits, skip tasks and edit directly.\n2. **Use the right tool**: on an empty scratchpad, use `insert_after`/`insert_before` with **no** `path` to seed the first binding (keep it small when possible); path-based `insert_after`/`insert_before` for adding bindings, `update_definition`/`update_body` for modifying existing code \xe2\x80\x94 prefer **many small** edits over one huge paste.\n3. **Manage context**: Collapsed definitions (\xe2\x8b\xb1) don\'t need to be expanded if tests or context already explain what they do. Expand only what you need.\n4. **Verify with probes**: After implementing, use `place_probe` to observe runtime values and confirm correctness. Clean up with `remove_probe` when done.\n5. **Check static errors**: After each edit, check the context for type errors. Fix them before moving on.\n6. **Communicate clearly**: Tell the user what was done and why, without referencing tool internals.\n',kHz=XE,kHA=aUk,kHB=mm,kHt=XE,kHu=aUk,kHv=mm,kHw=[0,g,[0,[0,i,XE],0]],kHx=[0,g,[0,[0,i,aUk],0]],kHy=[0,g,[0,[0,i,mm],0]],kHE=[0,0],kHI=[0,0],kHF=[0,0],kHG=[0,0],kHH=[0,0],kHC=[0,0],kHD=[0,0],kIr="editor-syntax",kIq=[0,1],kH_=[0,[18,[1,[0,[11,b,0],b]],[11,aY,0]],aZ],kH$=n_,kIa=[0,[18,[1,[0,0,m]],[2,0,[11,v,[17,[0,a,1,0],0]]]],w],kIb=[0,[17,0,0],u],kIc=[0,[12,59,[17,[0,a,1,0],0]],A],kId=ub,kIe=[0,[18,[1,[0,0,m]],[2,0,[11,v,[17,[0,a,1,0],0]]]],w],kIf=[0,[17,0,0],u],kIg=[0,[17,[0,a,1,0],[12,az,[17,0,0]]],a0],kIo=ub,kIp=n_,kIm=ub,kIn=n_,kIk=[0,ub],kIl=[0,n_],kIi=ub,kIj=n_,kHU=XY,kHJ=[0,[18,[1,[0,[11,b,0],b]],[11,aY,0]],aZ],kHK=n_,kHL=[0,[18,[1,[0,0,m]],[2,0,[11,v,[17,[0,a,1,0],0]]]],w],kHM=[0,[17,0,0],u],kHN=[0,[12,59,[17,[0,a,1,0],0]],A],kHO=pM,kHP=[0,[18,[1,[0,0,m]],[2,0,[11,v,[17,[0,a,1,0],0]]]],w],kHQ=[0,[17,0,0],u],kHR=[0,[12,59,[17,[0,a,1,0],0]],A],kHS=wd,kHT=[0,[18,[1,[0,0,m]],[2,0,[11,v,[17,[0,a,1,0],0]]]],w],kHV=[0,[17,0,0],u],kHW=[0,[17,[0,a,1,0],[12,az,[17,0,0]]],a0],kH7=wd,kH8=pM,kH9=n_,kH4=wd,kH5=pM,kH6=n_,kH1=[0,wd],kH2=[0,pM],kH3=[0,n_],kHY=wd,kHZ=pM,kH0=n_,kHX=dVB,kIh=dw_,kIs='\nReplaces the definition (the right-hand side of `=`, before `in`) of the binding at the given path.\nThis overwrites the ENTIRE definition \xe2\x80\x94 including any nested let bindings within it.\nWorks for both let bindings and module bindings (e.g. path "M" for module M = { ... }).\n\nParameters:\npath: string \xe2\x80\x94 slash-delimited path to the binding (e.g. "b", "M", or "outer/inner"). Inner defs use "outer/inner". A function-definition binding `let f(x, y) = ...` is addressed by its bare name: path "f" (parameters are not part of the path). If the same name appears more than once in the sibling chain (shadowing), the bare path is ambiguous and the tool errors, listing disambiguated forms; retry with "name#k" (k-th occurrence in program order, 1-based, e.g. "b#2"), or use a nested path when one binding sits inside another\'s definition.\ncode: string \xe2\x80\x94 the new definition code\n\nExample:\nGiven the program:\n```\nlet a = \xe2\x8b\xb1 in\nlet b = "hello" in\nlet c : Int = \xe2\x8b\xb1 in\n?\n```\nCalling update_definition(path="b", code="\\"world\\"") produces:\n```\nlet a = \xe2\x8b\xb1 in\nlet b = "world" in\nlet c : Int = \xe2\x8b\xb1 in\n?\n```\nNote: Only the definition changes. The pattern, body, and surrounding bindings are untouched.\n\nSyntax projectors (livelits): to keep a widget when overwriting the definition, include projector concrete syntax in `code`: `^^kind(expression)`. Examples: `^^slider(60)`, `^^sliderf(3.14)`, `^^check(true)`, `^^text("hello")`, `^^csv([])` (empty list only; import CSV in the UI), `^^card((Hearts, Ace))` (playing-card tuple or list of tuples\xe2\x80\x94not records). Without `^^`, the term is usually a plain literal and the projector is not preserved\xe2\x80\x94then use `place_syntax_projector` if needed (same term-shape rules as the editor menu).\n\nModule members: paths descend into module literals \xe2\x80\x94 `M/helper` (including type members: `M/Model`). Edit ONE member this way instead of re-emitting the whole module. Members end at their `;`, so they have no body: update_definition / update_pattern / update_binding_clause / delete_binding_clause / insert_before / insert_after all work at member paths, but update_body / delete_body error.\n',kIt=[0,[0,le,[0,P,[0,[0,b3,[0,i,jL]],[0,[0,ko,[0,P,[0,[0,ij,[0,P,[0,ogP,ogO]]],[0,[0,iB,[0,P,ogQ]],0]]]],[0,[0,h2,[0,g,[0,[0,i,ij],[0,[0,i,iB],0]]]],0]]]]],0],kIu=dW,kIv=[0,bi,[0,i,bfM]],kIw=e3,kIx=[0,b3,[0,i,e3]],kIy='\nReplaces the body (everything after `in`) of the binding at the given path.\nThe body is the rest of the program that follows this binding.\nWorks for both let bindings and module bindings (e.g. path "M" for module M = { ... }).\nModule MEMBERS (paths like "M/helper") have no body and are rejected \xe2\x80\x94 target the member\'s definition instead.\n\nParameters:\npath: string \xe2\x80\x94 slash-delimited path to the binding whose body to replace. Nested defs need ancestors (e.g. "wrap/is_odd"). Duplicate sibling names are ambiguous \xe2\x80\x94 disambiguate with "name#k" (k-th occurrence in program order, 1-based).\ncode: string \xe2\x80\x94 the new body code\n\nExample:\nGiven the program:\n```\nlet a = \xe2\x8b\xb1 in\nlet b = "hello" in\nlet c : Int = \xe2\x8b\xb1 in\n?\n```\nCalling update_body(path="b", code="a * a") produces:\n```\nlet a = \xe2\x8b\xb1 in\nlet b = "hello" in\na * a\n```\nNote: The body of "b" was `let c : Int = \xe2\x8b\xb1 in ?`. It is now replaced entirely with `a * a`.\nThe definition of "b" ("hello") is unchanged.\n',kIz=[0,[0,le,[0,P,[0,[0,b3,[0,i,jL]],[0,[0,ko,[0,P,[0,[0,ij,[0,P,[0,ogS,ogR]]],[0,[0,iB,[0,P,ogT]],0]]]],[0,[0,h2,[0,g,[0,[0,i,ij],[0,[0,i,iB],0]]]],0]]]]],0],kIA=dW,kIB=[0,bi,[0,i,bhj]],kIC=e3,kID=[0,b3,[0,i,e3]],kIE='\nRenames or changes the pattern (left-hand side of `=`) of the binding at the given path.\nAutomatically updates all use sites of the variable throughout the program.\nWorks at module MEMBER paths ("M/helper") \xe2\x80\x94 use sites inside the module are rewritten; a rename that would break references OUTSIDE the module (`M.member`) is rejected instead of applied.\n\nParameters:\npath: string \xe2\x80\x94 slash-delimited path to the binding to rename. Nested defs: use outer/inner. Duplicate sibling names are ambiguous \xe2\x80\x94 disambiguate with "name#k" (k-th occurrence in program order, 1-based).\ncode: string \xe2\x80\x94 the new pattern (may include type annotation)\n\nFor a function-definition binding `let f(x, y) = ...` the path is just "f" but the pattern is the whole head: rename with code "g(x, y)" (or rename a param with "f(x, z)"); call sites and param uses update automatically.\n\nThe rename is rejected (with an explanatory error) if:\n- the new name already occurs as a binder or variable reference within this binding\'s scope (it could capture existing references), or\n- the number of bound names changes (e.g. tuple pattern (x, y) \xe2\x86\x92 (a, b, c)), since old\xe2\x86\x92new use-site mapping would be ambiguous, or\n- rewriting the use sites would introduce new static errors.\n\nExample:\nGiven the program:\n```\nlet a = \xe2\x8b\xb1 in\nlet b = "hello" in\nlet c = b ++ " world" in\nc\n```\nCalling update_pattern(path="b", code="greeting : String") produces:\n```\nlet a = \xe2\x8b\xb1 in\nlet greeting : String = "hello" in\nlet c = greeting ++ " world" in\nc\n```\nNote: All references to "b" in the body are automatically renamed to "greeting".\n',kIF=[0,[0,le,[0,P,[0,[0,b3,[0,i,jL]],[0,[0,ko,[0,P,[0,[0,ij,[0,P,[0,ogV,ogU]]],[0,[0,iB,[0,P,ogW]],0]]]],[0,[0,h2,[0,g,[0,[0,i,ij],[0,[0,i,iB],0]]]],0]]]]],0],kIG=dW,kIH=[0,bi,[0,i,bsj]],kII=e3,kIJ=[0,b3,[0,i,e3]],kIK='\nReplaces the entire binding clause (from `let`/`type`/`module` through `in`, inclusive) at the given path.\nAt a module MEMBER path, replaces the whole member \xe2\x80\x94 write it without a trailing `;` (e.g. code="let w = 8").\nThis changes the pattern, definition, and delimiters \xe2\x80\x94 but NOT the body after the final `in`.\nThe code you provide should end with `in` (not include a final body expression).\nYou can introduce multiple bindings in one call (e.g., `let x = 1 in let y = 2 in`).\nWorks for let, type, and module bindings (e.g. path "M" for module M = { ... }).\n\nThe path is resolved against the program **as it exists before** this edit (the `code` argument does not change path lookup). Use the same paths you would get from reading the current buffer, not from hypothetical replacement text.\nBindings nested **inside another binding\'s definition** use paths like `outer/inner`. A chain `let a = ... in let b = ... in body` treats `a` and `b` as separate top-level path segments (`a`, `b`), not `a/b`, because `b` is in the outer body, not inside `a`\'s definition.\n\nTo update both the binding clause AND the body, call this tool followed by update_body.\n\nParameters:\npath: string \xe2\x80\x94 slash-delimited path to the binding to replace\ncode: string \xe2\x80\x94 the new binding clause(s), ending with `in`\n\nExample:\nGiven the program:\n```\nlet a = \xe2\x8b\xb1 in\nlet b = "hello" in\nlet c : Int = \xe2\x8b\xb1 in\n?\n```\nCalling update_binding_clause(path="b", code="let b : Int = 42 in let d = b + 1 in") produces:\n```\nlet a = \xe2\x8b\xb1 in\nlet b : Int = 42 in\nlet d = b + 1 in\nlet c : Int = \xe2\x8b\xb1 in\n?\n```\nNote: The body (everything after the original `in` of "b") is preserved \xe2\x80\x94 `let c` and `?` remain.\n',kIL=[0,[0,le,[0,P,[0,[0,b3,[0,i,jL]],[0,[0,ko,[0,P,[0,[0,ij,[0,P,[0,ogY,ogX]]],[0,[0,iB,[0,P,ogZ]],0]]]],[0,[0,h2,[0,g,[0,[0,i,ij],[0,[0,i,iB],0]]]],0]]]]],0],kIM=dW,kIN=[0,bi,[0,i,bjR]],kIO=e3,kIP=[0,b3,[0,i,e3]],kIQ='\nRemoves the entire binding (let...=...in, type...=...in, or module...=...in) at the given path.\nThe body that followed the binding is preserved and moves up.\nWorks for let, type, and module bindings (e.g. path "M" for module M = { ... }).\nAlso removes a single module MEMBER at a member path (e.g. "M/helper"), separator included.\n\nParameters:\npath: string \xe2\x80\x94 slash-delimited path to the binding to remove\n\nExample:\nGiven the program:\n```\nlet a = \xe2\x8b\xb1 in\nlet b = "hello" in\nlet c : Int = \xe2\x8b\xb1 in\n?\n```\nCalling delete_binding_clause(path="b") produces:\n```\nlet a = \xe2\x8b\xb1 in\nlet c : Int = \xe2\x8b\xb1 in\n?\n```\nNote: The binding `let b = "hello" in` is removed. Its body (`let c ... ?`) is preserved.\n',kIR=[0,[0,le,[0,P,[0,[0,b3,[0,i,jL]],[0,[0,ko,[0,P,[0,[0,ij,[0,P,[0,og1,og0]]],0]]],[0,[0,h2,[0,g,[0,[0,i,ij],0]]],0]]]]],0],kIS=dW,kIT=[0,bi,[0,i,bj_]],kIU=e3,kIV=[0,b3,[0,i,e3]],kIW='\nClears the body (everything after `in`) of the binding at the given path, replacing it with a hole (`?`).\nModule MEMBERS (paths like "M/helper") have no body and are rejected.\n\nParameters:\npath: string \xe2\x80\x94 slash-delimited path to the binding whose body to clear\n\nExample:\nGiven the program:\n```\nlet a = \xe2\x8b\xb1 in\nlet b = "hello" in\nlet c : Int = \xe2\x8b\xb1 in\nsome_expression\n```\nCalling delete_body(path="b") produces:\n```\nlet a = \xe2\x8b\xb1 in\nlet b = "hello" in\n?\n```\nNote: Everything after `in` for binding "b" (including `let c` and `some_expression`) is replaced with `?`.\n',kIX=[0,[0,le,[0,P,[0,[0,b3,[0,i,jL]],[0,[0,ko,[0,P,[0,[0,ij,[0,P,[0,og3,og2]]],0]]],[0,[0,h2,[0,g,[0,[0,i,ij],0]]],0]]]]],0],kIY=dW,kIZ=[0,bi,[0,i,buz]],kI0=e3,kI1=[0,b3,[0,i,e3]],kI2='\nInserts a new binding immediately after the binding at the given path.\nThe inserted code becomes part of the program between the target binding and its original body.\nAt a module MEMBER path (e.g. "M/helper"), inserts a new member after that member \xe2\x80\x94 write the member without a trailing `;` (e.g. code="let helper2 = 5"); the separator is added automatically.\n\nIf `path` is omitted, the code is inserted after the entire program (at the end). This is how you initialize an empty program: with the program at just `?`, call `insert_after(code="let x = 1 in")` (no path) to write the first binding.\n\nParameters:\npath: string (optional) \xe2\x80\x94 slash-delimited path to the binding after which to insert. Omit to insert after the whole program.\ncode: string \xe2\x80\x94 the code to insert (typically a let...in or type...in binding)\n\nExample:\nGiven the program:\n```\nlet a = 10 in\nlet b = "hello" in\n?\n```\nCalling insert_after(path="a", code="let doubled = a * 2 in") produces:\n```\nlet a = 10 in\nlet doubled = a * 2 in\nlet b = "hello" in\n?\n```\nNote: The new binding is inserted between "a" and "b". The rest of the program is preserved.\n\nInitialization example (empty program):\nCurrent program: `?`\nCalling insert_after(code="let x = 42 in") produces:\n```\nlet x = 42 in\n?\n```\n'